<header><p class="st-kicker">emirate · Arabia and the Gulf</p><h1 class="st-title">Sheikhdom of Kuwait</h1><p class="st-meta">1752 CE – 1961 CE (approx.) · capital Kuwait City</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Al Sabah trading town at the head of the Gulf that passed from pearling and shipbuilding to oil wealth under British protection.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Clans of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bani-utbah">Bani Utbah</a> settled at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-kuwait">Kuwait</a> in the early 18th century under the protection of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/banu-khalid-emirate">Banu Khalid Emirate</a>. Around 1752 they chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sabah-i-bin-jaber">Sabah I</a> as their leader, founding the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-sabah">Al Sabah</a> dynasty that still rules.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-kuwait">Kuwait</a> was a merchant town of pearl divers, dhow builders and long-distance traders to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>, governed by the ruler with the consent of leading merchant families. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mubarak-al-sabah">Mubarak al-Sabah</a>, who took power in 1896, signed the Anglo-Kuwaiti Agreement of 1899, placing Kuwait under British protection against Ottoman claims. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uqair-protocol">Uqair conference</a> of 1922 set its borders with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sultanate-of-nejd">Sultanate of Nejd</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Oil was discovered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgan-field">Burgan</a> in 1938 and exported from 1946, transforming the economy under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdullah-al-salim-al-sabah">Abdullah Al-Salim Al-Sabah</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> ended the 1899 agreement on 19 June 1961, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/state-of-kuwait">State of Kuwait</a> became independent.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/sheikhdom-of-kuwait.jpg" alt="Sheikhdom of Kuwait" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Tcfc2349 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Kuwait_(1940-1961).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1752 CE – 1961 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>emirate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kuwait City</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Sabah I, Mubarak al-Sabah, Abdullah Al-Salim Al-Sabah</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Arabic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/kuwait">Kuwait</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kuwait, Emirate of Kuwait</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1856&amp;focus=sheikhdom-of-kuwait" aria-label="Sheikhdom of Kuwait on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5930 1104 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Sheikhdom of Kuwait lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6250 1360L6314 1501M6250 1360L5709 970M6250 1360L4997 651M6250 1360L6250 1360"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6314 1501h0M5709 970h0M4997 651h0M6250 1360h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6250 1360h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6250 1360h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Kuwait City. <a href="/atlas?year=1856&amp;focus=sheikhdom-of-kuwait">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
